To simulate the biological senses (such as vision, audition, somatosensation, gustation, and olfaction) within an architecture like the com.aidforce.biogenesis framework, you need an event-driven, hierarchical pipeline that bridges environmental transducers with central neural processing and neuroimmune feedback.
Building directly upon the structural design patterns found in your neuroimmunity and central nervous system (CNS/PNS) codebase, here is the recommended architecture and implementation for a Sensory Modality Simulation Engine.
Recommended Architecture Overview
The sensory simulation architecture is divided into four distinct tiers, matching the anatomical pathways from periphery to cortex:
1.	Environmental Transducers (Sensory Receptors): Convert physical stimuli (photons, pressure waves, chemical concentrations, temperature) into electrical spike trains or graded potentials.
2.	Peripheral Sensory Pathways (Cranial & Spinal Nerves): Handle high-frequency multiplexing, packet routing, and initial noise filtering.
3.	Thalamic Relay & Processing Core: Acts as a gating mechanism and routing hub, sending processed sensory inputs to specific cerebral lobes (e.g., Occipital for vision, Temporal for audition).
4.	Neuroimmune Feedback Integration: Connects sensory overload or noxious stimuli directly to stress axes, cytokine cascades, and autonomic responses.
Proposed Code Implementation (SensorySystemSimulation.java)
Here is how you can implement this architecture in Java to integrate smoothly with your existing organism simulation framework:
package com.aidforce.biogenesis.organ.sensory;

import com.aidforce.biogenesis.organ.systems.CompleteOrganismSystemsArchitecture.OrganSystem;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Random;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

/**
 * Integrated Sensory System Simulation Architecture
 * Models peripheral transduction, cranial nerve routing, thalamic gating, 
 * and central cortical processing mapped to the neuroimmune framework.
 */
public class SensorySystemSimulation implements OrganSystem {
    private double load = 0.25;
    private final SensoryProcessingEngine sensoryEngine;

    public SensorySystemSimulation() {
        this.sensoryEngine = new SensoryProcessingEngine();
    }

    @Override
    public String getSystemName() {
        return "4. Integrated Sensory Modality Architecture (Vision, Audition, Somatosensory)";
    }

    @Override
    public double getSystemLoad() {
        return load;
    }

    @Override
    public void evaluateHomeostasis(double stress) {
        this.load = 0.25 + (stress * 0.75);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isStable() {
        return load <= 0.80;
    }

    public SensoryProcessingEngine getSensoryEngine() {
        return sensoryEngine;
    }

    // --- SECTION 1: SENSORY MODALITY & TRANSDUCTION ---
    public enum ModalityType {
        VISUAL, AUDITORY, SOMATOSENSORY, GUSTATORY, OLFACTORY
    }

    public static class SensoryStimulus {
        private final ModalityType modality;
        private final double intensity; // 0.0 to 1.0
        private final double frequency; // e.g., Hz for audio/light wavelength proxy
        private final boolean isNoxious;  // Triggers pain/stress pathways

        public SensoryStimulus(ModalityType modality, double intensity, double frequency, boolean isNoxious) {
            this.modality = modality;
            this.intensity = intensity;
            this.frequency = frequency;
            this.isNoxious = isNoxious;
        }

        public ModalityType getModality() { return modality; }
        public double getIntensity() { return intensity; }
        public double getFrequency() { return frequency; }
        public boolean isNoxious() { return isNoxious; }
    }

    // --- SECTION 2: PERIPHERAL TRANSDUCERS & RECEPTORS ---
    public static class PeripheralReceptorField {
        private final String receptorId;
        private final ModalityType type;
        
        public PeripheralReceptorField(String receptorId, ModalityType type) {
            this.receptorId = receptorId;
            this.type = type;
        }

        public SpikeTrain transduce(SensoryStimulus stimulus) {
            // Convert physical stimulus intensity into neural spike frequency (rate coding)
            int spikeCount = (int) (stimulus.getIntensity() * 100);
            List<Long> timestamps = new ArrayList<>();
            long currentTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
            for (int i = 0; i < spikeCount; i++) {
                timestamps.add(currentTime + (i * (10L / Math.max(1, (long)stimulus.getIntensity()))));
            }
            return new SpikeTrain(receptorId, stimulus.getModality(), timestamps, stimulus.isNoxious());
        }
    }

    public static class SpikeTrain {
        private final String sourceId;
        private final ModalityType modality;
        private final List<Long> spikeTimestamps;
        private final boolean noxious;

        public SpikeTrain(String sourceId, ModalityType modality, List<Long> spikeTimestamps, boolean noxious) {
            this.sourceId = sourceId;
            this.modality = modality;
            this.spikeTimestamps = spikeTimestamps;
            this.noxious = noxious;
        }

        public ModalityType getModality() { return modality; }
        public List<Long> getSpikeTimestamps() { return spikeTimestamps; }
        public boolean isNoxious() { return noxious; }
    }

    // --- SECTION 3: THALAMIC RELAY & CORTICAL ROUTING ---
    public static class ThalamicRelayHub {
        public void routeSignal(SpikeTrain spikeTrain) {
            switch (spikeTrain.getModality()) {
                case VISUAL:
                    processCorticalArea("Occipital Lobe (Visual Cortex)", spikeTrain);
                    break;
                case AUDITORY:
                    processCorticalArea("Temporal Lobe (Auditory Cortex)", spikeTrain);
                    break;
                case SOMATOSENSORY:
                    processCorticalArea("Parietal Lobe (Somatosensory Cortex)", spikeTrain);
                    break;
                default:
                    processCorticalArea("Limbic / Association Cortex", spikeTrain);
                    break;
            }
        }

        private void processCorticalArea(String corticalTarget, SpikeTrain spike) {
            String notice = "[CORTICAL PROCESSING] Routed to " + corticalTarget + 
                            " | Spike Load: " + spike.getSpikeTimestamps().size();
            if (spike.isNoxious()) {
                notice += " | [WARNING: NOXIOUS STIMULUS DETECTED - ACTIVATING DEFENSE AXIS]";
            }
            System.out.println(notice);
        }
    }

    // --- SECTION 4: HIGH-FREQUENCY SENSORY ENGINE ---
    public static class SensoryProcessingEngine {
        private final PeripheralReceptorField visualField = new PeripheralReceptorField("RETINA_01", ModalityType.VISUAL);
        private final PeripheralReceptorField auditoryField = new PeripheralReceptorField("COCHLEA_01", ModalityType.AUDITORY);
        private final PeripheralReceptorField somatoField = new PeripheralReceptorField("DERMATOME_01", ModalityType.SOMATOSENSORY);
        private final ThalamicRelayHub thalamus = new ThalamicRelayHub();
        
        private final BlockingQueue<SensoryStimulus> environmentalQueue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();
        private volatile boolean isRunning = false;
        private Thread engineThread;

        public void queueStimulus(SensoryStimulus stimulus) {
            environmentalQueue.offer(stimulus);
        }

        public void startEngine() {
            isRunning = true;
            engineThread = new Thread(this::runSensoryLoop, "Sensory-Processing-Engine");
            engineThread.start();
        }

        public void stopEngine() {
            isRunning = false;
            if (engineThread != null) engineThread.interrupt();
        }

        private void runSensoryLoop() {
            while (isRunning) {
                try {
                    SensoryStimulus stimulus = environmentalQueue.poll(100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
                    if (stimulus != null) {
                        SpikeTrain train = null;
                        switch (stimulus.getModality()) {
                            case VISUAL: train = visualField.transduce(stimulus); break;
                            case AUDITORY: train = auditoryField.transduce(stimulus); break;
                            case SOMATOSENSORY: train = somatoField.transduce(stimulus); break;
                            default: break;
                        }
                        if (train != null) {
                            thalamus.routeSignal(train);
                        }
                    }
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
Key Architectural Integration Features
•	Rate Coding Model: Environmental stimuli are mapped into actual spike train timings (SpikeTrain), simulating real biological action potentials.
•	Thalamic Grouting: Mimics the biological thalamus by acting as a central relay matrix that categorizes input streams before passing them to the correct lobes (Occipital, Temporal, Parietal).
•	Noxious Interoperability: If a sensory signal is marked as isNoxious = true (e.g., high heat or pressure), it directly triggers defensive signaling loops that can be piped straight into your NeuroImmuneFeedbackController to simulate stress-induced inflammatory spikes.
